    "evidence_text": "A Candidate Gene Approach Identifies the TRAF1/C5 Region as a Risk Factor for Rheumatoid Arthritis.\n\nPLoS Med 4(9): e278.\n\nAllele A refers to the susceptibility alleles as given in column seven.\n\nUltrasound imaging for lumbar punctures and epidural catheterisations: systematic review and meta-analysis.\n\nFive studies evaluated lumbar punctures and nine evaluated epidural catheterisations.\n\nSix of 624 procedures conducted in the ultrasound group failed; 44 of 610 procedures in the control group failed.\n\nUltrasound may be a useful adjunct for these procedures.\n\nEthnographic study of incidence and severity of intravenous drug errors.\n\nPARTICIPANTS Nurses who prepared and administered intravenous drugs.   \n\n\nMAIN OUTCOME MEASURES Number, type, and clinical importance of errors.   \n\n\nRESULTS 249 errors were identified.\n\nMost errors occurred when giving bolus doses or making up drugs that required multiple step preparation.   \n\n\nCONCLUSIONS The rate of intravenous drug errors was high.\n\nOperable non-small cell lung cancer diagnosed by transpleural techniques : do they affect relapse and prognosis?\n\nDESIGN A retrospective study.   \n\n\nSETTING National referral hospital.   \n\n\nThis is also confirmed as an independent prognostic factor in a multivariate analysis.   \n\n\nThe effectiveness of right heart catheterization in the initial care of critically ill patients. SUPPORT Investigators.\n\nDESIGN Prospective cohort study.   \n\n\nSETTING Five US teaching hospitals between 1989 and 1994.   \n\nA propensity score for RHC was constructed using multivariable logistic regression.\n\nThese findings were all confirmed by multivariable modeling techniques.\n\nPatients with higher baseline probability of surviving 2 months had the highest relative risk of death following RHC.\n\nThe cause of this apparent lack of benefit is unclear.\n\nThe total number of patients sustaining complications was 33 for an overall complication rate of 16.5%.\n\nThere were two deaths in this series, for a mortality rate of 1%.\n\nNo patient who entered the operating room neurologically intact sustained a permanent neurological deficit postoperatively.\n\nEffect of clinical guidelines on medical practice: a systematic review of rigorous evaluations.\n\nHowever, the size of the improvements in performance varied considerably.\n\nExtravasation injuries on regional neonatal units.\n\nCommon treatments were exposing wounds to the air, infiltration with hyaluronidase and saline, and occlusive dressings.\n\nFor studies of the LP technique, data on the intervention and the outcome were extracted.\n\nDATA SYNTHESIS We found 15 randomized trials.\n\nA random-effects model was used for quantitative synthesis.\n\nCONCLUSIONS These data suggest that small-gauge, atraumatic needles may decrease the risk of headache after diagnostic LP.\n\nReinsertion of the stylet before needle removal should occur and patients do not require bed rest after the procedure.\n\nRole of Computerized Physician Order Entry Systems in Facilitating Medication Errors\n\nThe premise was that simply notifying the physician would increase the use of appropriate prophylactic measures.\n\nThe computer alert decreased the risk of deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism by 41%.\n\nThere are 2 lessons urologists can learn from this study.",
